            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Multiplies every element of in 1 by every element of in 2, laying the products out as a table: row i, column j holds in 1's i-th value times in 2's j-th.\n\nTwo curves make a surface this way: a horizontal profile times a vertical one gives a 2-D shape - a spotlight, a window, a weighting over a grid. It is also how one pattern is given a different amplitude per channel (gains times a signal), and the building block of covariance and of many matrix methods.\n\nA stack of vectors (N x n and N x m) gives N tables at once. The answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.outer\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe values for the rows. Every input calculates and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe values for the columns, held: send them first.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nouter product:\nAn n x m table (or N of them).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 2 3 times 1 10 (held): a 3 x 2 table, 1 2 3 down the first column and 10 20 30 down the second.\n\nRELATED:\nt.dot adds the products of matching entries into one number; t.outer keeps every product. t.matmul multiplies whole matrices. np.outer is the numpy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 860,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Multiplies every element of in 1 by every element of in 2, laying the products out as a table: row i, column j holds in 1's i-th value times in 2's j-th.\n\nTwo curves make a surface this way: a horizontal profile times a vertical one gives a 2-D shape - a spotlight, a window, a weighting over a grid. It is also how one pattern is given a different amplitude per channel (gains times a signal), and the building block of covariance and of many matrix methods.\n\nA stack of vectors (N x n and N x m) gives N tables at once. The answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.outer\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe values for the rows. Every input calculates and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe values for the columns, held: send them first.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nouter product:\nAn n x m table (or N of them).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 2 3 times 1 10 (held): a 3 x 2 table, 1 2 3 down the first column and 10 20 30 down the second.\n\nRELATED:\nt.dot adds the products of matching entries into one number; t.outer keeps every product. t.matmul multiplies whole matrices. np.outer is the numpy version."
        },
